<!-- Definition and symptoms -->
'''Asthma''' is a common [[chronic (medicine)|long-term]] [[inflammation|inflammatory]] disease of the airways. It is characterized by variable and recurring symptoms and reduced lung function. Symptoms include episodes of [[wheezing]], [[coughing]], chest tightness, and [[shortness of breath]]. A sudden worsening of asthma symptoms sometimes called an '''asthma attack''' or an '''asthma exacerbation''' can occur when [[allergens]], [[pollen]], dust, or other particles are inhaled into the lungs, causing the [[Bronchiole|bronchioles]] to constrict and produce mucus, which then restricts [[oxygen]] flow to the [[pulmonary alveoli|alveoli]]. These may occur a few times a day or a few times per week. Depending on the person, asthma symptoms may become worse at night or with exercise.

<!-- Cause and diagnosis -->
Asthma is thought to be caused by a combination of [[Genetics|genetic]] and [[environmental factor]]s. Environmental factors include exposure to [[air pollution]] and [[allergen]]s. Other potential triggers include medications such as [[aspirin]] and [[beta blockers]]. Diagnosis is usually based on the pattern of symptoms, response to therapy over time, and [[spirometry]] lung function testing. Asthma is classified according to the amount of medication required to control symptoms or mechanisms underlying the condition.

<!-- Prevention and treatment -->
There is no known cure for asthma, but it can be controlled. Symptoms can be prevented by avoiding triggers, such as [[allergens]] and respiratory [[irritation|irritants]], and suppressed with the use of inhaled [[corticosteroid]]s. [[Long-acting beta-adrenoceptor agonist|Long-acting beta agonists]] (LABA) or [[antileukotriene agent]]s may be used in addition to inhaled corticosteroids if asthma symptoms remain uncontrolled. Treatment of rapidly worsening symptoms is usually with an inhaled short-acting [[Beta2-adrenergic agonist|beta{{sub|2}} agonist]] such as [[salbutamol]], corticosteroids taken by mouth, and [[oxygen therapy]]. In very severe cases, intravenous corticosteroids and hospitalization may be required.

<!-- Epidemiology, history, and society -->
In 2023, asthma affected an estimated 363{{nbsp}}million people worldwide and caused 442,000{{nbsp}}deaths. Most asthma-related deaths occur in low- and lower-middle-income countries. Asthma often begins in [[Childhood asthma|childhood]]. It was recognized as early as [[ancient Egypt]]. The word ''asthma'' is from the Greek {{lang|grc|ἆσθμα}} ({{transliteration|grc|âsthma}}), which means 'panting'.
