Hives including chronic spontaneous hives can be a complication and symptom of a parasitic infection, such as [[blastocystosis]] and [[strongyloidiasis]] among others.<ref name="pmid26648083">{{Cite journal |last=Kolkhir |first=P. |last2=Balakirski |first2=G. |last3=Merk |first3=HF. |last4=Olisova |first4=O. |last5=Maurer |first5=M. |date=December 2015 |title=Chronic spontaneous urticaria and internal parasites – a systematic review. |journal=Allergy |volume=71 |issue=3 |pages=308–322 |doi=10.1111/all.12818 |pmid=26648083 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

The rash that develops from [[poison ivy]], [[poison oak]], and [[poison sumac]] contact is commonly mistaken for urticaria. This rash is caused by contact with [[urushiol]] and results in a form of [[contact dermatitis]] called [[urushiol-induced contact dermatitis]]. Urushiol is spread by contact but can be washed off with a strong grease- or oil-dissolving detergent and cool water and rubbing ointments.

{{Main|Cholinergic urticaria}}

[[Cholinergic urticaria]] (CU) is one of the [[physical urticaria]] which is provoked during sweating events such as exercise, bathing, staying in a heated environment, or emotional stress. The hives produced are typically smaller than classic hives and are generally shorter-lasting.<ref name="CU2009REF2">{{Cite journal |last=Moore-Robinson |first=Miriam |last2=Warin |first2=Robert P. |year=1968 |title=Some Clikical Aspects of Cholhstergic Urticaria |journal=British Journal of Dermatology |volume=80 |issue=12 |pages=794–9 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2133.1968.tb11948.x |pmid=5706797 |s2cid=58415911}}</ref><ref name="CU2009REF3">{{Cite journal |last=Hirschmann |first=J. V. |last2=Lawlor |first2=F |last3=English |first3=JS |last4=Louback |first4=JB |last5=Winkelmann |first5=RK |last6=Greaves |first6=MW |year=1987 |title=Cholinergic Urticaria - A Clinical and Histologic Study |journal=Archives of Dermatology |volume=123 |issue=4 |pages=462–7 |doi=10.1001/archderm.1987.01660280064024 |pmid=3827277}}</ref>

Multiple subtypes have been elucidated, each of which require distinct treatment.<ref name="PCU">{{Cite journal |last=Nakamizo |first=S. |last2=Egawa |first2=G. |last3=Miyachi |first3=Y. |last4=Kabashima |first4=K. |year=2012 |title=Cholinergic urticaria: Pathogenesis-based categorization and its treatment options |url=https://zenodo.org/record/891804 |journal=Journal of the European Academy of Dermatology and Venereology |volume=26 |issue=1 |pages=114–6 |doi=10.1111/j.1468-3083.2011.04017.x |pmid=21371134 |s2cid=35802279 |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name="PCU2012">{{Cite journal |last=Bito |first=Toshinori |last2=Sawada |first2=Yu |last3=Tokura |first3=Yoshiki |year=2012 |title=Pathogenesis of Cholinergic Urticaria in Relation to Sweating |journal=Allergology International |volume=61 |issue=4 |pages=539–44 |doi=10.2332/allergolint.12-RAI-0485 |pmid=23093795 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

{{Further|Cold urticaria}}
The cold type of urticaria is caused by exposure of the skin to extreme cold, damp and windy conditions; it occurs in two forms. The rare form is hereditary and becomes evident as hives all over the body 9 to 18 hours after cold exposure. The common form of cold urticaria demonstrates itself with the rapid onset of hives on the face, neck, or hands after exposure to cold. Cold urticaria is common and lasts for an average of five to six years. The population most affected is young adults, between 18 and 25 years old. Many people with the condition also have dermographism and cholinergic hives.{{citation needed|date=August 2013}}

